From caf-talk Caf Aug 30 03:13:28 1993
From: TK0JUT1@NIU.BITNET (Jim Thomas              , tk0jut1@mvs.cso.niu.edu)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: The problem with the FERPA ruling
Date: 30 Aug 1993 03:13:27 -0400
Message-ID: <199308300713.AA04543@eff.org>

Christopher Burian writes:

    >My opinion: this is a "good thing," as long as it's NOT applied
    >retroactively.  I think that would be an undue hardship on libraries,
    >and on legitimate research.

Although implementing the Dept of Ed's FERPA interpretation might seem
like a minor inconvenience, the implications would reflect a radical
philosophical shift.  For starters:

1) If a thesis/dissertation is considered a private document, then the
procedures that generate the document, such as the customary public
defense, could also be closed. Since both the procudures and the final
product are intended as a public demonstration of the ability to
conduct independent scholarship, hopefully of benefit to others, then
the purpose of the exercise is lost.

2) The public nature of theses/dissertations provide at least minimal
accountabilty for both faculty and students. Professors can often be
assessed on the basis of the aggregate performance of their students'
products.

3) In some disciplines, such as art, photography, or music, it is not
uncommon for institutions hiring new faculty to request examples of
*students'* work to assess teaching qualifications.  Although I find
this a questionable practice, sealing students' efforts would
proscribe the practice.

To my mind, simply adding another level of paper work by "requesting"
that the student sign a waiver is unacceptable.  The fundamental norm
of public work ought not be left to the discretion of an individual.
If the FERPA interpretation stands, then the basic premises underlying
graduate research dissolve.  If theses/dissertations become private
student records unavailable to others for use, critique and
accountability, I, for one, would advocate removing them from the
curriculum altogether, because much of their purpose would be lost.

Jim Thomas

