Subject: what to do about a net abuser?
Date: 31 Aug 1993 22:15:24 GMT
Message-ID: <260ihs$fmi@gazette.bcm.tmc.edu>

Threats of legal action? Calls to sys admins trying to make trouble
with people's employers? Complaints to the FCC? No, these aren't
what I'm suggesting doing--these are what the guy *did*. (Well, I'm
not sure how far he's gotten with the complaints to the FCC. Why
would the FCC even care?)

A number of regular posters on the newsgroup misc.kids have recently
had quite a lot of trouble from a certain Carl Jones. His errors
range from posting private e-mail ("Please don't clutter my e-mail
with personal messages, as I prefer to reserve it for more important
matters"--paraphrase--his sysadmin did get him to think better of
that idea, after some effort) through shamelessly advertising his
books repeatedly, and apparently (according to a number of folks
who've been following his postings on misc.kids) sharing another 
user's account so that he could pursue these activities less
obviously. Either he or the other user whose account he may have 
shared posted some potentially dangerous medical advice, which 
peeved several people, also.

No biggie, send him private e-mail, and if that fails, send a
polite message to his system administrator asking him to explain
the system to the user, right? Except it didn't work--it just got
him angry. His system administrator actually told Mr. Jones
the names of some (but not all) of the individuals who'd sent him
messages asking him to talk to him. Nasty phone calls ensued-- 
lengthy harangues of system administrators, threatening
legal action (without even the slimmest of legal grounds), 
attempting to get the individuals with whom he was angry in trouble 
with their employers--and now he's threatening to contact the FCC.

Nobody lost their jobs, and he hasn't actually showed up on any
doorsteps with a shotgun or anything. The system administrators
he's called have been marvels of forbearance as they put up with
his tirades. Trying to cut him off from net participation seems too
much like censorship--it's the outside-of-the-net activities that
have been the problem, after all. So what is the appropriate thing
to do? Probably wait it out, since surely the FCC won't take any 
interest at all. Any opinions? 

Advice appreciated.

Paula Burch
pburch@bcm.tmc.edu

